ZTEXT001000013c1620a89580000000
Zero archive: original ZPACK001 indexed container

Implementation: tools/archive.zero. Built from Zero by the original Zero compiler.
No ZIP/TAR library, external archive program, compression codec or subprocess is
used. The Linux tool is zero-archive; Windows uses zero-archive.exe.

Commands:
  zero-archive make MANIFEST.ztext NEW.zpack
  zero-archive verify FILE.zpack
  zero-archive list FILE.zpack
  zero-archive read FILE.zpack NEW_DIRECTORY

make reads listed regular files relative to the process working directory, not
relative to the manifest's directory. Output is created exclusively; an existing
file is preserved. Source files must stay unchanged during packing. Manifest
paths can include nested directories and UTF-8 names. Empty files and arbitrary
binary contents are preserved. The manifest order determines listing order.

A manifest is a validated ZTEXT001 envelope containing one path per line.
Ordinary lines describe data files. Prefix a line with x| to explicitly mark an
executable file; the marker is not part of the archived path. For example:
  README.ztext
  tools/archive.zero
  x|native/zero-archive

The current manifest uses LF, accepts an optional CR before LF, and may omit the
last newline. Empty manifests produce empty archives; blank lines are rejected.
The x| marker is unambiguous because pipe is forbidden inside stored paths.
Packing does not infer permissions from filename extensions or execute files.

read extracts into a new single-component directory in the current directory.
The directory must not already exist, even if empty. Every nested parent is
created by this invocation, then every member is created exclusively. Linux data
files use0600 and explicit executable files0700. Windows creates ordinary files;
executability follows the host's normal file rules. The tool never runs members.
Directories have no separate archive entries. Empty directories, timestamps,
ownership, ACLs, symlinks and other special filesystem objects are not stored.

Before extraction creates anything, the complete bounded archive is read and its
header, index, every path, every offset, every size and all checksums are checked.
Offsets must be contiguous in manifest order; gaps, overlap and trailing bytes
are rejected. Output and extracted members are flushed, closed, reopened and
compared byte for byte with the in-memory validated data, including exact EOF.
On failure the tool attempts to remove only files and directories it created.
If cleanup cannot complete, it reports that an incomplete owned folder remains.
An existing archive, destination folder or destination symlink is never replaced.

Stored paths are relative UTF-8 with forward-slash separators. Absolute paths,
empty components, . or .. components, drive prefixes, backslashes, NUL, controls,
Windows wildcard/stream punctuation, components with leading spaces or trailing
spaces/dots, and reserved Windows device names are rejected. This includes the
CON/PRN/AUX/NUL, COM/LPT1..9 and superscript1/2/3, CONIN$ and CONOUT$ aliases.
Exact and ASCII-case duplicate paths, file/directory prefix conflicts and
ASCII-case parent aliases are rejected. Unicode case/normalization comparison is
not implemented. A Unicode alias accepted on Linux may collide on Windows;
exclusive creation then fails and the owned extraction tree is cleaned up.

There must be no concurrent writer changing the source tree, archive output or
new extraction tree while the command runs. The host API does not provide an
openat-style directory handle boundary. Initial destination ownership plus
strict paths protect ordinary extraction, not hostile concurrent path swaps.
The manifest is trusted to select source files: intermediate source-directory
symlinks follow native host path resolution; final source symlinks are refused.

Bounds:
  64MiB maximum complete archive, including header and index.
  4096 files; 255 UTF-8 bytes per stored path; 32 path components.
  ZTEXT001 manifest payload at most1MiB; header adds32 bytes.
  Bounded native workspace68165632 bytes. All file I/O uses <=65536-byte chunks.
The in-memory design makes pre-extraction validation complete within these bounds.

Binary format, version1; all integers unsigned little-endian64-bit:
Header64 bytes:
  offset0: ASCII ZPACK001
  offset8: version1
  offset16: file count
  offset24: index byte count, exactly file count*288
  offset32: complete archive byte count
  offset40: FNV1a32 of the entire index, upper32 bits zero
  offset48 and56: reserved zero
Index starts at64, each entry288 bytes:
  offset0..255: UTF-8 relative path, NUL terminated and zero padded
  offset256: absolute payload offset within the archive
  offset264: payload byte count
  offset272: payload FNV1a32, upper32 bits zero
  offset280: flags0 for data or1 for explicitly executable
Payload follows the index, in entry order, with no padding between files.
FNV1a32 starts2166136261; for each byte use ((hash XOR byte)*16777619) modulo2^32.
Checksums detect accidental changes; they are not authentication or encryption.
